      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1209,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound with the formula **$H_2O$**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. The Structure\nWater is a **polar molecule**. Because oxygen is more \"thirsty\" for electrons than hydrogen, the oxygen end of the molecule has a slight negative charge, while the hydrogen ends have a slight positive charge. This polarity allows water to stick to itself (cohesion) and to other substances (adhesion), which is why water forms droplets and flows.\n\n### 2. The Three States of Matter\nWater is one of the few substances on Earth that naturally exists in all three physical states:\n*   **Solid:** As ice (which is less dense than liquid water and floats).\n*   **Liquid:** The state we use for drinking, washing, and swimming.\n*   **Gas:** As steam or water vapor (which rises into the atmosphere).\n\n### 3. Key Properties\n*   **The Universal Solvent:** Water is excellent at dissolving other substances. It can dissolve more types of materials than any other liquid. This is why it is the primary transport medium in the human body (carrying nutrients to cells) and in the environment (moving minerals through soil).\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick together very strongly at the surface, creating a kind of \"skin.\" This allows small insects to walk across puddles without sinking.\n*   **High Specific Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat without getting hot. This helps regulate the Earth's temperature (oceans act as heat sinks) and keeps human bodies stable.\n\n### 4. Abundance and Importance\nWater covers about 71% of the Earth's surface. It is the foundation of life as we know it. All known forms of life require water to survive, using it for chemical reactions, temperature regulation, and as a medium for transporting nutrients and waste."
